</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Wen Ji
</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) enable powerful cross-modal inference but impose significant computational and latency burdens, posing severe challenges for deployment in resource-constrained environments. In this paper, we propose MoA-Off, an adaptive heterogeneous modality-aware offloading framework with edge-cloud collaboration for efficient MLLM inference. MoA-Off introduces a lightweight heterogeneous modality-aware module that estimates the complexity of heterogeneous inputs through multi-dimensional feature analysis. Then, an adaptive edge-cloud collaborative offloading strategy is proposed that dynamically schedules workloads between edge and cloud based on modality-aware complexity scores and real-time system states. The experimental results demonstrate that MoA-Off can achieve over 30% reduction in latency and 30%–65% decrease in resource overhead while maintaining competitive accuracy compared to traditional approaches.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Index Terms: </h6>Multimodal LLM, edge-cloud collaboration, adaptive offloading, inference optimization

</div><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_address"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">address: </span> <sup id="id1.1" class="ltx_sup">1</sup>Institute of Computing Technology, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing, China 
<br class="ltx_break"><sup id="id1.2" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>Institute of AI for Industries, Nanjing, China 
<br class="ltx_break"><sup id="id1.3" class="ltx_sup">3</sup>University of Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing, China 
<br class="ltx_break"><sup id="id1.4" class="ltx_sup">4</sup>Peng Cheng Laboratory, Shenzhen, China</span></span></span>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) have recently demonstrated remarkable capabilities in integrating visual, auditory, and textual information, enabling unified reasoning across heterogeneous data sources <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. as shown in Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ MoA-Off: Adaptive Heterogeneous Modality-Aware Offloading with Edge-Cloud Collaboration for Efficient Multimodal LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. These models are increasingly deployed in applications ranging from intelligent assistants to real-time perception systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. However, their impressive performance comes at the cost of substantial computational and memory demands <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, which pose severe challenges in latency-sensitive and resource-constrained environments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. Pure cloud-based solutions, while powerful, often suffer from excessive transmission latency, bandwidth overhead, and potential privacy risks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. In contrast, edge-only approaches provide lower response time and better locality but are fundamentally limited by restricted computing capacity<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, leading to degraded performance on complex multimodal tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To address these limitations, recent research has explored edge–cloud collaborative inference <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, in which lightweight pre-processing is conducted on edge devices and computation-intensive reasoning is offloaded to the cloud. Such hybrid solutions balance efficiency and accuracy by distributing workloads across heterogeneous resources. Nevertheless, existing collaborative frameworks typically adopt uniform offloading policies, treating multimodal inputs without considering their intrinsic heterogeneity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. In practice, different modalities exhibit distinct computational characteristics, communication costs, and sensitivity to inference accuracy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. Ignoring these differences often results in suboptimal allocation of tasks, inefficient resource utilization, and inconsistent user experience <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.16995v1/figure1.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/238;" width="476" height="238" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>The examples of MLLM.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we present MoA-Off, an adaptive modality-aware offloading framework for efficient MLLM inference with edge–cloud collaboration. Our approach introduces modality-specific complexity into the offloading process, thereby improving both system efficiency and inference performance. The main contributions are as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We develop a lightweight heterogeneous modality-aware module, which estimates the complexity of image and text inputs through efficient feature extraction, while maintaining minimal overhead on edge devices.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose an adaptive edge-cloud collaborative offloading policy. This policy enables fine-grained scheduling, ensuring that resource-intensive modalities are offloaded to the cloud for accurate reasoning, while lightweight inputs are retained on the edge for low-latency processing.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct extensive experiments on multimodal benchmark datasets. The experimental results demonstrate that MoA-Off achieves over 30% reduction in latency while maintaining competitive accuracy, alongside a 30%–65% decrease in resource overhead, compared to traditional approaches.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Multimodal LLM.</span> MLLM extends conventional text-based LLMs by integrating heterogeneous modalities such as text, images, audio, and video into a unified reasoning framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>. Formally, given an input set of modalities <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{x_{t},x_{v},x_{a}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{x_{t},x_{v},x_{a}}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to text, vision, and audio, the model constructs modality embeddings <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{m}=f_{m}(x_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{m}=f_{m}(x_{m})</annotation></semantics></math> through respective encoders <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{m}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{m}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>, and then aligns them into a common latent space via a projection function <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{m}=g\left(h_{m}\right),\quad m\in\{t,v,a\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{m}=g\left(h_{m}\right),\quad m\in\{t,v,a\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This formulation empowers MLLMs to handle complex tasks such as multimodal question answering, captioning, and grounded reasoning, yet also results in significant computational and memory demands, making efficient deployment a non-trivial challenge.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Edge-Cloud Architecture.</span> Edge-cloud architectures provide a hierarchical computing paradigm that distributes inference workloads across resource-constrained edge devices and powerful cloud servers, thereby balancing latency, energy efficiency, and computational capacity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">yang2021intelligent212121</span>]</cite>. This division enables low-latency responses while leveraging the scalability of the cloud for complex reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>. Such a collaborative design mitigates the limitations of standalone edge inference while reducing cloud dependency compared to purely centralized solutions, making it especially suitable for real-time multimodal applications under dynamic network and resource conditions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>The Proposed Method</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose MoA-Off, an adaptive heterogeneous modality-aware offloading framework designed to enhance the efficiency of MLLM inference by edge–cloud collaboration, as illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3 The Proposed Method ‣ MoA-Off: Adaptive Heterogeneous Modality-Aware Offloading with Edge-Cloud Collaboration for Efficient Multimodal LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. It primarily consists of two parts: (1) <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Lightweight Heterogeneous Modality-Aware</span>, and (2) <span id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Adaptive Edge-Cloud Collaborative Offloading</span>. The core idea of MoA-Off is to exploit the heterogeneous characteristics of different modalities and dynamically allocate computational workloads between edge and cloud.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.16995v1/figure2.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/294;" width="476" height="294" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>The overview of the proposed MoA-Off framework.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Lightweight Heterogeneous Modality-Aware</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The lightweight heterogeneous modality-aware module is designed to efficiently identify the type and estimate the complexity of each input modality on the edge device. Its primary objective is to provide lightweight yet reliable complexity scores, which serve as the basis for the adaptive offloading policy. To ensure practicality, all metrics are derived from fast, single-pass feature extraction techniques <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, making them suitable for real-time deployment in resource-constrained environments.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.1 </span>Image Complexity</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For an input image <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in[0,255]^{H\times W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0,255</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in[0,255]^{H\times W}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=HW" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=HW</annotation></semantics></math> pixels, the image complexity is quantified as a weighted combination of several lightweight indicators that capture different aspects of visual information. Specifically, we consider resolution scale, edge density, entropy-based texture, and sharpness. The overall complexity is computed as a weighted sum: <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\mathrm{img}}=w_{\mathrm{res}}C_{\mathrm{res}}+w_{\mathrm{edge}}C_{\mathrm{edge}}+w_{\mathrm{ent}}C_{\mathrm{ent}}+w_{\mathrm{lap}}C_{\mathrm{lap}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>img</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>res</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>res</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>edge</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>edge</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>ent</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>ent</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>lap</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>lap</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\mathrm{img}}=w_{\mathrm{res}}C_{\mathrm{res}}+w_{\mathrm{edge}}C_{\mathrm{edge}}+w_{\mathrm{ent}}C_{\mathrm{ent}}+w_{\mathrm{lap}}C_{\mathrm{lap}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the weights <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> are non-negative and normalized to sum to one. Each indicator is defined as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Resolution scale.</span>
Higher-resolution images typically require more computation. We normalize image size relative to a reference resolution <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H_{0},W_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H_{0},W_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\mathrm{res}}=\min\left(1,\frac{HW}{H_{0}W_{0}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>res</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\mathrm{res}}=\min\left(1,\frac{HW}{H_{0}W_{0}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Edge density.</span>
Images with dense edges usually contain richer structural information and thus higher processing costs. We measure the average Sobel gradient magnitude and normalize it:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\text{edge }}=\operatorname{clip}\left(\frac{\bar{G}-P_{5}(G)}{P_{95}(G)-P_{5}(G)+\epsilon},0,1\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mtext>edge </mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>clip</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>95</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\text{edge }}=\operatorname{clip}\left(\frac{\bar{G}-P_{5}(G)}{P_{95}(G)-P_{5}(G)+\epsilon},0,1\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Entropy-based texture.</span>
Texture richness is approximated by image entropy, derived from the gray-level histogram:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H(I)=-\sum_{k=0}^{255}p_{k}\log p_{k},\quad C_{\mathrm{ent}}=\frac{H(I)}{\log 256}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mn>255</mn></munderover><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>ent</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(I)=-\sum_{k=0}^{255}p_{k}\log p_{k},\quad C_{\mathrm{ent}}=\frac{H(I)}{\log 256},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is the normalized frequency of gray-level <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sharpness.</span>
Sharp or detailed images are more demanding to process. We use the variance of the Laplacian operator as a sharpness indicator:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\text{lap }}=\operatorname{clip}\left(\frac{\operatorname{Var}\left(\nabla^{2}I\right)-P_{5}}{P_{95}-P_{5}+\epsilon},0,1\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mtext>lap </mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>clip</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>5</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>95</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>5</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\text{lap }}=\operatorname{clip}\left(\frac{\operatorname{Var}\left(\nabla^{2}I\right)-P_{5}}{P_{95}-P_{5}+\epsilon},0,1\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{5}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{95}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>95</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{95}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>-th and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="95" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>95</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">95</annotation></semantics></math>-th percentiles of the Laplacian variance across a calibration set.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.2 </span>Text Complexity</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Text complexity reflects the processing overhead introduced by long sequences, entity density, and context dependency <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>.
The final text complexity score is computed as: <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\text{text }}=\beta_{L}C_{L}+\beta_{\text{ner }}C_{\text{ner }}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mtext>text </mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mtext>ner </mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mtext>ner </mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\text{text }}=\beta_{L}C_{L}+\beta_{\text{ner }}C_{\text{ner }}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{\text{ner}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mtext>ner</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{\text{ner}}</annotation></semantics></math> are weights.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Token length.</span>
Longer sequences lead to quadratic growth in transformer computation. We normalize token count <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> relative to a threshold <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{L}=\min\left(1,\frac{L}{L_{0}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mi>L</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{L}=\min\left(1,\frac{L}{L_{0}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Entity or numeric density.</span>
The presence of named entities, numerical expressions, or specialized terminology often increases semantic reasoning complexity. We measure this factor by computing the average number of entities per sentence: <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\mathrm{ner}}=\min\left(1,\frac{E/S}{\gamma}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>ner</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>γ</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\mathrm{ner}}=\min\left(1,\frac{E/S}{\gamma}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of entities and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of sentences, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is a scaling constant.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Adaptive Edge-Cloud Collaborative Offloading</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Building upon the modality-specific complexity estimation introduced in <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Section 3.1</span>, we design an adaptive offloading strategy that dynamically determines whether each modality should be processed locally at the edge or offloaded to the cloud. The key objective is to minimize latency and resource overhead while maintaining inference accuracy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For each modality <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{i}\in{\text{text},\text{image}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mtext>text</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo><mtext>image</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i}\in{\text{text},\text{image}}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> denote the estimated complexity score from the modality perception module. The system state is represented by <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=(\ell,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=(\ell,b)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> is the current edge load and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> is the available network bandwidth. The offloading decision <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as:</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}=\begin{cases}\text{ edge },&amp;c_{i}\leq\tau_{m_{i}}\wedge\ell\leq\ell_{\max}\wedge b\leq\beta,\\
\text{ cloud, }&amp;\text{ otherwise, }\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext> edge </mtext><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>∧</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>∧</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext> cloud, </mtext></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext> otherwise, </mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}=\begin{cases}\text{ edge },&amp;c_{i}\leq\tau_{m_{i}}\wedge\ell\leq\ell_{\max}\wedge b\leq\beta,\\
\text{ cloud, }&amp;\text{ otherwise, }\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{m_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{m_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the modality-specific complexity threshold, <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> is the maximum tolerable edge utilization, and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> is a bandwidth limit.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In multimodal tasks, different modalities often exhibit divergent levels of complexity and resource demand. Instead of enforcing a uniform offloading decision for the entire input, our strategy employs a per-modality routing mechanism that enables partial offloading and fine-grained scheduling. For instance, in an image and text query, the image, characterized by high computational and bandwidth requirements, can be offloaded to the cloud, while the accompanying short text is processed locally on the edge for immediate response. Formally, the decision vector is defined as</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=\pi(c_{1},c_{2},\ldots,c_{k},s)\in{\text{edge},\text{cloud}}^{k}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mtext>edge</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mtext>cloud</mtext><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=\pi(c_{1},c_{2},\ldots,c_{k},s)\in{\text{edge},\text{cloud}}^{k},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the number of modalities in the input, <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the complexity score of modality <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> represents the system state (including edge load and network bandwidth), and <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is the adaptive scheduling policy function that integrates modality-aware thresholds with system-level dynamics.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We utilize an NVIDIA A100 (40 GB) GPU as the cloud server and an NVIDIA 3090 (24 GB) as the edge device. Qwen2-VL-2B model is deployed on the edge, while Qwen-2.5-VL-7B model is deployed in the cloud <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>. The evaluation datasets are VQAv2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> and MMBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, we randomly selected 5,000 images from the validation set for testing. The network bandwidth is set to 200 Mbps, 300 Mbps, and 400 Mbps, respectively. The weights for image complexity and text complexity are set to their average values, with the complexity threshold set to 0.5. The baseline methods include (1) <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Cloud-only</span> (Qwen-2.5-VL-7B), (2) <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Edge-only</span> (Qwen2-VL-2B), and (3) <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">PerLLM</span> (an edge-cloud collaboration solution). Evaluation metrics include accuracy, End-to-end latency, and resource overhead.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Main Results and Analysis</h3>

<section id="S4.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.1 </span>Accuracy Comparison</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.2.1 Accuracy Comparison ‣ 4.2 Main Results and Analysis ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ MoA-Off: Adaptive Heterogeneous Modality-Aware Offloading with Edge-Cloud Collaboration for Efficient Multimodal LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, the proposed MoA-Off framework achieves highly competitive performance. Critically, it attains an overall accuracy that is comparable to the cloud-only approach with a negligible performance penalty of merely <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textless" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textless</annotation></semantics></math>0.4%. Furthermore, our method substantially outperforms the edge-only and PerLLM by a considerable margin of over 4.8%-16.8% in absolute accuracy. These results highlight the effectiveness of the proposed MoA-Off framework.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Accuracy (%) comparison results.</figcaption>
<div id="S4.T1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:124.8pt;vertical-align:-59.6pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(17.7pt,-6.4pt) scale(1.11425435003037,1.11425435003037) ;">
<table id="S4.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T1.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Method</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T1.2.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cloud-only</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T1.2.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Edge-only</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T1.2.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PerLLM</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.1.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T1.2.1.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MoA-Off</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" colspan="5"><span id="S4.T1.2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">VQAv2 Dataset</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.2.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
